window.__finlexDocLoaded(10149,{"id":10149,"articles":[{"id":1000639,"no":"Overview","heading":"Scope & Global Benchmark Status (2023 Edition)","text":"Endorsed by G20 Leaders in September 2023, the G20/OECD Principles of Corporate Governance serve as the global standard for sound corporate governance and one of the Financial Stability Board's (FSB) Key Standards for Sound Financial Systems. Structured into six chapters, the 2023 edition updates guidance for capital markets, company groups, digital technologies, bondholder rights, and sustainability-related risks.","seq":0},{"id":1000640,"no":"Chapters I–III","heading":"Framework, Shareholder Rights & Institutional Investors","text":"[Chapter I — Effective Corporate Governance Framework]\n• Transparent and fair markets, efficient resource allocation, clear division of supervisory/regulatory responsibilities, and cross-border regulatory cooperation.\n\n[Chapter II — Rights & Equitable Treatment of Shareholders]\n• Basic ownership rights, participation and voting in general meetings (including remote/hybrid digital meetings), disclosure of disproportionate control/capital structures, and fair approval/disclosure of material related-party transactions (aligning with IAS 24).\n• Clear rules for corporate control markets, takeovers, and protection of minority shareholders in company groups.\n\n[Chapter III — Institutional Investors, Stock Markets & Intermediaries]\n• Stewardship and voting policy disclosures by institutional investors, management of conflicts of interest by proxy advisors, analysts, rating agencies, and ESG rating/data providers.","seq":1},{"id":1000641,"no":"Chapters IV–VI","heading":"Disclosure, Board Responsibilities & Sustainability (New in 2023)","text":"[Chapter IV — Disclosure and Transparency]\n• Timely and accurate disclosure of financial and operating results, major share ownership and beneficial ownership, remuneration, related-party transactions, and foreseeable risk factors; high-quality accounting (IFRS) and independent annual external audit (ISAs).\n\n[Chapter V — Responsibilities of the Board]\n• Duty of care and duty of loyalty; guiding corporate strategy, overseeing internal control and risk management (aligning with COSO ICIF / ERM), selecting/monitoring key executives, ensuring integrity of accounting/reporting systems, and maintaining board independence and audit committee effectiveness.\n\n[Chapter VI — Sustainability and Resilience (New 2023 Chapter)]\n• Consistent, comparable, and reliable sustainability-related disclosures (interoperable with IFRS S1 / S2), independent assurance over sustainability information (ISSA 5000), board oversight of climate and stakeholder risks, and dialogue between companies, shareholders, and stakeholders.","seq":2},{"id":1000642,"no":"Official Access","heading":"Official Publication & Full Report Entry","text":"Official Publication Page: https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/g20-oecd-principles-of-corporate-governance-2023_ed750b30-en.html\nFull Online Report: https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/g20-oecd-principles-of-corporate-governance-2023_ed750b30-en/full-report.html\nStatus: in_force (2023 Edition).","seq":3}]});
